{
  "thought_process": "This is the first tuning round. Current parameters: P=0.4, I=0.005, D=0.0. Setpoint is ~40.0. Looking at the time series, the Input values are oscillating between ~46 and ~52, which means the system is currently well above the setpoint of 40. The error values show the system oscillating around zero with amplitude ~10 degrees above setpoint. The reported overshoot is 30.2% and steady-state error is 8.78, meaning the system is sitting far above the setpoint with significant oscillation. The PWM output ranges from about -3.9 to +4.3, which is very small. The system appears to be oscillating with a period of roughly 200 samples * 110ms = about 2.2 seconds based on the zero crossings. The input is hovering around 49-52 degrees while the setpoint is 40 degrees - so the system is ~9-12 degrees above setpoint consistently. The steady-state error of 8.78 indicates the integrator is not doing enough work, or P is too low to drive the output to correct the error. Wait - looking more carefully: the error is defined as setpoint - input = 40 - input. So when input=48, error = 40-48 = -8, which matches the data. The system is ABOVE setpoint consistently. The PWM output is negative (trying to reduce temperature/value) but not strongly enough. With P=0.4 and error=-8, PWM contribution from P = 0.4 * (-8) = -3.2, which matches roughly. The I=0.005 is very small and won't contribute much. The system has a large negative steady-state error (output too high), meaning the system needs stronger corrective action. Since we're in Phase 1 (P adjustment), and P=0.4 is clearly too small to bring the system to setpoint effectively, I need to increase P. The oscillation with 30.2% overshoot at P=0.4 is concerning - but looking at the data, the oscillation amplitude is about 3 degrees peak-to-peak around a mean of ~50 (10 above setpoint). This looks like the system is oscillating due to the inherent dynamics rather than P being too large. Actually the overshoot of 30.2% might be from a previous step response. The current oscillation with 2 zero crossings and the system orbiting around setpoint+10 suggests the integrator hasn't wound up enough. Given conservative hardware safe tuning, I should increase P moderately. Current P=0.4, guardrail says keep within 3x = max 1.2. I'll increase P to 0.8 (2x increase) to strengthen the proportional response. Keep I at 0.005 (very small, essentially phase 1). D stays 0.",
  "analysis_summary": "System is oscillating ~10 units above setpoint (input ~49-52, setpoint=40). With P=0.4, the proportional correction (P*error ≈ -3 to -4 PWM) is insufficient to drive the system to setpoint. Steady-state error of 8.78 confirms the proportional gain is too weak. Overshoot 30.2% and 2 zero-crossings indicate underdamped oscillation. This is Phase 1 (P tuning). P needs to be increased to generate stronger corrective output. I=0.005 is negligibly small and appropriate for Phase 1. No D needed yet.",
  "tuning_action": "Increase P from 0.4 to 0.8 (2x increase, within 3x guardrail). This doubles the proportional correction force to better drive the system toward setpoint. Keep I=0.005 unchanged (near-zero for Phase 1). D remains 0. Observe whether the system converges closer to setpoint and whether oscillation amplitude reduces or worsens.",
  "p": 0.8,
  "i": 0.005,
  "d": 0.0,
  "status": "TUNING"
}

